INT. WEDDING TENT - GENERATOR TRAILER - NIGHT

A cramped steel trailer behind the marquee. Cables, fuel cans, a dim bulb. The generator thrums. Through the thin wall, a band plays a slow song.

NAOMI (late 20s, bridesmaid dress, boots) sits on a crate with a flask. She's been crying, a little, and has wiped it off.

HENRIK (30s, rolled shirtsleeves, tie loosened) slips in, shutting the door. He doesn't see her. He leans his forehead against the wall.

She coughs.

He spins.

HENRIK
Jesus.

NAOMI
Sorry. I was here first.

HENRIK
You're a bridesmaid.

NAOMI
You're the groom's brother.

He nods. She holds out the flask. He takes it. Drinks. Hands it back.

HENRIK
Why are you in the generator trailer?

NAOMI
It's the only quiet place. You?

HENRIK
Same.

They sit in silence. The band shifts to a waltz.

NAOMI
You gave a nice toast.

HENRIK
It was a lie.

NAOMI
All toasts are lies.

He laughs. She laughs. Then it fades.

HENRIK
Do you know the bride well?

NAOMI
Twelve years. Since college.

HENRIK
Then you know why she's marrying him.

NAOMI
(carefully)
I know why she thinks she is.

He studies her. She holds the look. Outside, a spotlight sweeps the tent wall.

HENRIK
(to himself)
I should go back.

NAOMI
You should.

Neither moves. He sits on a fuel can across from her. Closer than before. She offers the flask again. Their fingers touch. She doesn't pull away.

HENRIK
Naomi.

NAOMI
You remember my name.

HENRIK
It's on the place cards.

NAOMI
Is that all it is?

He leans in. She leans in. A heartbeat from a kiss.

The trailer door bangs open. GRAN IMELDA (80s, silk shawl, cane) fills the frame, with BEST MAN DEWEY (30s, drunk, tux jacket on backwards) on her arm.

GRAN IMELDA
(to the whole tent)
Found them!

DEWEY
(slurring)
The bride's been looking for you, Henrik. She says you're late for the photograph.

Naomi and Henrik spring apart.

GRAN IMELDA
(to Naomi, pointed)
And you, dear. She's looking for you, too.

Naomi stands, smooths her dress. Henrik takes his tie in both hands.

NAOMI
(low, to Henrik)
Don't let this be the end of it.

HENRIK
(low)
It isn't.

They walk past Gran Imelda together into the lights. Imelda watches them go, then turns to Dewey.

GRAN IMELDA
Young man. Never tell the groom's mother what you saw.

DEWEY
(muttering)
I didn't see anything.
